In a close-up at the deep vertical crack in the textured coral reef wall, Noah, a little boy with blonde wavy ear-length hair with bangs, blue eyes, wearing a short-sleeve shimmering orange scale-embossed top and a shimmering orange merman tail, plunges his right arm deep into the crevice, his fingers curled tight around the thin brown cord of the glowing pearlescent singing shell necklace, his brow furrowed with strain as he pulls backward. Beside him, Emma, a little girl with brown wavy ear-length hair in a ponytail, brown eyes, freckles, wearing a shimmering green scale-patterned top and green mermaid tail, braces her palms against the outer coral edges of the opening, watching the tug-of-war where Oskar, the small reddish-orange octopus with round blue eyes, anchors multiple suction-cupped arms against the deep rock interior, holding fast to the shell's opposite end.

